# The image the glibc Linux addons are built in, and the glibc version is the
# whole reason it exists.
#
# A binary cannot link against symbol versions its build sysroot does not have,
# so the build base *is* the floor. Measured on `ubuntu-latest` the addon came
# out demanding GLIBC_2.35 and GLIBCXX_3.4.30, and failed to load on Rocky 9,
# Amazon Linux 2023 and everything older -- while being published as
# `linux-x64-gnu`, a name that claims every glibc Linux on x64. This image is
# how that name becomes true.
#
# What the floor has to clear, measured from the images rather than assumed:
#
#   RHEL / Rocky / Alma 8            glibc 2.28   GLIBCXX 3.4.25
#   AWS Lambda / Amazon Linux 2023   glibc 2.34   GLIBCXX 3.4.33
#   RHEL / Rocky / Alma 9            glibc 2.34   GLIBCXX 3.4.29
#   Ubuntu 22.04                     glibc 2.35
#   Debian 12 / node:22              glibc 2.36
#
# # Why manylinux rather than a base we assemble
#
# `manylinux_2_28_x86_64` **is** AlmaLinux 8.10 -- glibc 2.28, the lowest row
# above -- and it already carries gcc-toolset-14 as the default `gcc`/`g++`
# plus a current cmake. Those are the two hardest parts of assembling this by
# hand, and PyPA maintains them for the same reason we would: shipping portable
# native binaries.
#
# It also generalises, which matters more with four Linux targets. The same
# layer applies to `manylinux_2_28_aarch64`, and `musllinux_1_2_*` is the same
# shape for the musl pair -- Alpine 3.22 with gcc, g++ and cmake already
# present. One thin layer, four bases we do not maintain.
#
# **This is where we diverge from `meo-skia-canvas`, deliberately.** Their
# image starts from bare `almalinux:8`, whose default GCC is 8.5 -- which has no
# `<bit>`, so Skia fails on `SkMathPriv.h`, so they install clang and point it
# at gcc-toolset's headers. Starting from manylinux the default compiler is
# already GCC 14, so that entire chain is unnecessary here. Their reasoning was
# right for their starting point and does not transfer.
# The base is an `ARG` so one file serves both architectures. The x86_64 and
# aarch64 manylinux images are the same distribution at the same glibc, and
# nothing in this layer is architecture-specific except the assembler below.
ARG BASE=quay.io/pypa/manylinux_2_28_x86_64
FROM ${BASE}

# What the base does not carry.
#
# `nasm` is for libaom, which assembles its hot paths on x86 rather than
# compiling them and raises a FATAL_ERROR when it can find neither nasm nor
# yasm. **It is x86-only, measured rather than assumed**: `libaom-sys` builds
# on aarch64 in this image with no assembler installed at all, because libaom
# uses NEON intrinsics there and compiles them rather than assembling them.
#
# It is an `ARG` rather than a second Dockerfile, and that is the whole reason
# it is one: adding a target should cost a row in `TARGETS` and nothing else,
# and a forked image for the sake of one package is exactly the kind of second
# edit that makes the claim untrue. `--build-arg ASSEMBLER=` builds the aarch64
# image from this same file.
#
# `gperf` is fontconfig's build dependency. `ninja-build` and a current `meson`
# are fontconfig's build system -- EL8 ships meson 0.58, which is too old for
# fontconfig 2.17, so it comes from pip as it does in `install-fontlibs.sh`.
ARG ASSEMBLER=nasm
RUN dnf -y install --setopt=install_weak_deps=False \
      ${ASSEMBLER} gperf ninja-build make \
 && dnf clean all \
 && python3 -m venv /opt/meson \
 && /opt/meson/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir meson
ENV PATH="/opt/meson/bin:${PATH}"

# The font libraries, built static and installed with NO shared object.
#
# # Why they are built rather than installed
#
# Skia is compiled without `embed-freetype`, so `rust-skia` links the system
# freetype and fontconfig -- and its `linux.rs` probes pkg-config for both and
# falls back to the bare names when the probe fails, silently. Whatever those
# names resolve to is what the addon then needs at load time, and measured,
# `libfontconfig.so.1` and `libfreetype.so.6` are absent from every image in
# the table above, `node:22-slim` included. The addon fails there before glibc
# is even a question.
#
# **Static linking the distribution's own archives is not the fix, and the
# measurement is the reason.** With the shared objects hidden, `-lfontconfig`
# does resolve to `libfontconfig.a` and both libraries are absorbed -- zero
# NEEDED entries, no patched dependency required. But Ubuntu's archive drags
# its own dependencies onto the link line and the binary comes out needing
# `libexpat`, `libbz2`, `libpng16` and `libbrotlidec` instead. Three of those
# four are missing from every image above. Two missing libraries traded for
# four is a regression, and it would have passed a naive "is fontconfig gone
# from NEEDED" check.
#
# So the libraries are built here with their optional dependencies switched
# off, which is the part that makes the static link worth having.
COPY install-fontlibs.sh /opt/install-fontlibs.sh
RUN bash /opt/install-fontlibs.sh && rm -f /opt/install-fontlibs.sh

# `--static` on the probe, so pkg-config hands rust-skia the transitive list
# rather than just the two names. Without it the link fails on fontconfig's own
# undefined references -- measured, `XML_GetCurrentLineNumber` first.
ENV PKG_CONFIG_ALL_STATIC=1

# libstdc++ pinned to an old baseline, and **the linker is the trap**.
#
# # What `-static-libstdc++` does here, measured rather than assumed
#
# This comment said "absorbed rather than depended on", and the artefact says
# otherwise: `NEEDED libstdc++.so.6`, and 53 C++ symbols resolved from it at
# load. Both halves of that were worth measuring rather than softening.
#
# The flag is doing most of the work. **396 `std::` symbols are defined in the
# binary's own `.text`** -- linked in from `libstdc++.a` rather than looked up.
#
# It cannot remove the dependency, because it is not the only thing on the link
# line asking for libstdc++. `rust-skia` emits `cargo:rustc-link-lib=stdc++`
# unconditionally -- its `link_libraries` opens with `vec!["stdc++"]`
# (`build_support/platform/linux.rs:37`) -- so rustc puts `-lstdc++` in the
# dynamic section regardless of what the compiler driver was told. The static
# archive satisfies what it reaches first and the shared object answers the
# rest.
#
# **What the flag buys is the version of the residue, and that is the whole
# point.** What is left is old: the newest thing required is GLIBCXX_3.4.21,
# which is GCC 5.1's C++11 string and stream ABI -- `std::__cxx11::
# basic_stringstream`, `basic_stringbuf`, `getline`, `locale::name`. A GCC 14
# link without this flag would demand far newer, and 3.4.21 sits under
# AlmaLinux 8's own 3.4.25, which is the oldest platform this image targets.
#
# So: **static where it reaches, dynamic to a 2015 baseline.** Every image in
# the acceptance table carries a libstdc++ newer than that.
#
# # `g++` here, where `meo-skia-canvas` uses `clang++`, and that is not a
# # simplification of their design
#
# Their image installs clang and points it at gcc-toolset's headers because it
# starts from bare `almalinux:8`, whose default compiler is GCC 8.5 -- which has
# no `<bit>`, so Skia fails on `SkMathPriv.h`. **That problem does not exist
# here.** manylinux already puts gcc-toolset-14 on the path as the default
# `gcc`/`g++`, so the compiler is GCC 14 before we do anything, and the clang
# chain would be working around a fault we do not have.
#
# This is written down because the divergence looks like a mistake from the
# outside: a reader comparing the two files sees us not doing what a working
# upstream does, and the obvious "fix" is to put clang back. The reason we can
# use `g++` is the base, and if the base ever changes this reasoning goes with
# it.
#
# rustc links through `cc`, the C driver, which does not act on
# `-static-libstdc++` and drops it without a word. `meo-skia-canvas` shipped a
# binary reporting GLIBCXX_3.4.21 -- under every ceiling -- that still failed to
# load on `undefined symbol: _M_replace_cold`, a GCC 12 symbol carrying no
# version tag at all. Pointing the linker at `g++` makes it a C++ link and the
# flag takes.
#
# **A version ceiling structurally cannot catch this**, which is why the
# acceptance test is loading the binary and not reading its symbol versions.
ENV RUSTFLAGS="-C linker=g++ -C link-arg=-static-libstdc++"

ARG RUST=1.98.0
RUN curl -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh -s -- -y --profile minimal --default-toolchain "$RUST"
ENV PATH="/root/.cargo/bin:${PATH}"

WORKDIR /src
